Yes, an HTTP 403 Forbidden status code is the most common sign that a firewall or Web Application Firewall (WAF) has blocked your request.
When you are fuzzing or testing a target and suddenly get a 403 Forbidden response, it usually means your payload tripped a security signature.

How to Tell the Difference: WAF Block vs. Normal 403

Not all 403 errors are from firewalls. Sometimes a website naturally returns a 403 if you try to visit an admin page without logging in. Here is how you spot a firewall block:

Indicator	Normal Web App 403	Firewall / WAF 403 Block
Response Headers	Standard server headers (e.g., Server: Apache).	Shows firewall names (e.g., Server: cloudflare, X-Gateway, Server: Akamai).
Response Body	Simple text like "Login Required" or custom site branding.	Displays security block pages, challenge screens (CAPTCHAs), or a unique Reference ID string.
Trigger Event	Happens immediately when clicking a private link.	Only triggers the exact millisecond you type an exploit character (like ' or <).


🚀 What to do next when you hit a Firewall 403

When you see a firewall 403, do not give up. This is actually great news for a bug bounty hunter. It proves that the page or parameter you are testing is connected to something important that the company is actively trying to hide.
To break past it, you start your mutation workflow:
1. Isolate the character: Strip your payload down. Send just a single quote '. If it gets blocked, you know the firewall is watching that specific character.
2. Change the Content-Type: If you sent the request as a standard web form (application/x-www-form-urlencoded), try converting the request to JSON (application/json) inside Burp Suite. Sometimes firewalls only check form fields and ignore JSON payloads entirely.
3. Use the 403 Bypass Headers: Some firewalls are configured to let traffic pass if it looks like it comes from a trusted internal source. Use Burp Suite to inject headers like:
	• X-Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1
	• X-Originating-IP: 127.0.0.1
	• X-Remote-IP: 127.0.0.1
